    {"label":"Six Responsible AI Principles","parentTopic":"Responsible AI Principles","objective":"1.1","keyword":"Microsoft names six: FAIRNESS (similar people, similar outcomes), RELIABILITY & SAFETY (works consistently and safely, including unexpected conditions), PRIVACY & SECURITY (protect data, control access), INCLUSIVENESS (usable and beneficial for people of all abilities and backgrounds), TRANSPARENCY (people can understand how it works and its limits), ACCOUNTABILITY (humans own and answer for the system). Scenario test: name the principle a situation supports or violates."},
    {"label":"Temperature vs Max Tokens vs System Prompt","parentTopic":"Model Deployment & Parameters","objective":"1.2","keyword":"TEMPERATURE = randomness (lower = more consistent, higher = more varied). MAX TOKENS = maximum response length, not quality. SYSTEM PROMPT = role, scope and rules for the conversation. Wrong-lever trap: raising max tokens does not make answers more accurate; lowering temperature does not restrict topics."},
    {"label":"Agent = Model + Instructions + Tools","parentTopic":"Building Single Agents in Foundry","objective":"2.1","keyword":"A Microsoft Foundry agent is a model, instructions (role, scope, what to refuse) and the tools it may call to act (for example searching knowledge or calling business functions). Single chat completion = one prompt in, one answer out. Multi-step work that takes actions = agent. Fix scope problems in the instructions first."},
    {"label":"Deployment Name in Foundry API Calls","parentTopic":"Foundry SDK Chat Clients","objective":"2.1","keyword":"Calling a model deployed in Foundry from Python: the model argument is the DEPLOYMENT NAME you chose; messages carry roles (system = behaviour, user = request); with the Chat Completions API the reply text is response.choices[0].message.content (the Responses API uses response.output_text). A model from the catalog must be deployed before an app can call it; the chat playground tests it with no code."},
    {"label":"Pick the Right Foundry Tool for the Workload","parentTopic":"Information Extraction Scenarios","objective":"1.3","keyword":"TEXT (sentiment, entities, key phrases, summarization, PII) = Azure Language. TRANSCRIPTION or SPOKEN OUTPUT as the end product = Azure Speech in Foundry Tools; a spoken question answered directly by one model = multimodal model with audio input. QUESTIONS ABOUT AN IMAGE or spoken prompts to one model = multimodal model. NEW IMAGES from a prompt = image generation model. STRUCTURED FIELDS from documents, images, audio or video = Azure Content Understanding with a field schema."},
    {"label":"Multimodal Model vs Dedicated Service","parentTopic":"Visual Input with Multimodal Models","objective":"2.3","keyword":"A multimodal model takes text plus images (and some take audio) in the same request and reasons over them, so the image goes in the user message next to the instruction. Choose a dedicated service when you need a specific, repeatable output: transcription (Speech), structured fields (Content Understanding), or text analytics (Language)."}
